WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:02.750
ALEX J. POLLOCK: Good evening, ladies and
gentlemen.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.979
We’re going to start by singing “America
the Beautiful,” two verses.

00:00:06.979 --> 00:00:10.870
You will see the words up on these big screens.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:16.810
So if we could all stand and join in singing
together.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:33.260
O beautiful for spacious skies
For amber waves of grain

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:41.750
For purple mountain majesties
Above the fruited plain

00:00:41.750 --> 00:01:03.670
America, America
God shed his grace on thee

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:17.750
And crown thy good with brotherhood
From sea to shining sea

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:25.420
O beautiful for pilgrim feet
Whose stern impassioned stress

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:34.520
A thoroughfare of freedom beat
Across the wilderness

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:44.220
America, America
God mend thine every flaw

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:56.680
Confirm thy soul in self-control
Thy liberty in law.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:03.200
ARTHUR BROOKS: Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.860
It’s my pleasure to welcome all of you to
the American Enterprise Institute’s annual

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:08.860
gala.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.150
I’m Arthur Brooks, president of AEI.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:19.480
On behalf of our trustees, our board of academic
advisers, our 200 scholars and staff, we’re

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.510
honored to share this evening with you tonight.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.770
Our annual gala is always a special evening.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.870
It’s time to honor the great work that our
scholars and fellows have done over the past

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:30.870
year.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:35.120
It’s a time to enjoy the company of friends
and colleagues in this incredibly beautiful

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.130
setting, and, most importantly, it’s the
occasion on which we present the Irving Kristol

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.650
Award, AEI’s highest honor.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.520
But tonight is a particularly special one
for us at AEI because tonight we celebrate

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:59.069
AEI’s 75th anniversary.

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:01.020
This is a signal milestone for AEI.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.550
It means we fought for free enterprise and
American greatness for almost a third of the

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.379
republic’s history.

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:13.569
As we pause to reflect on the first 75 years,
we also rededicate ourselves to the timeless

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:17.060
principles and our enduring cause.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:23.319
Maximizing liberty, increasing individual
opportunity, strengthening free enterprise,

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:24.450
and American leadership.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.540
These aren’t just our values as an Institute.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.819
They’re the bedrock of the American project.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.280
They’re why America remains a beacon to
the world.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.599
We believe in these principles because we
believe in the idea and the ideal of America

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:46.590
and because we know that free enterprise creates
unmatched opportunities for human flourishing

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.519
and that American leadership is an act of
global brotherhood.

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:56.190
We also know that these principles urgently
need defenders, defenders who can explain

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:00.810
why freedom, opportunity, and enterprise not
only make us better off, but they make us

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.640
better people — people willing and able
to share the prosperity and opportunity with

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.890
those who need it most here and around the
world.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:14.580
There are many in our country today who fear
that America’s best days are behind us,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.870
that we’re fading into just another giant
social democracy.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:24.990
But I look out over the faces in this room,
at men and women utterly committed to limited

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:31.370
government, but an unlimited America, and
I feel confident about our nation’s future.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:37.110
At the American Enterprise Institute, we see
ourselves as conservators of a tradition,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:41.630
building on the wisdom of our intellectual
forefathers and strengthening that which is

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.650
best in our country.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:49.270
With every generation, AEI cultivates and
develops what has come before seeking to improve

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.530
our great American institutions.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:58.780
Now we realize that even the greatest intellectual
insights are of scant use without policy entrepreneurs

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.500
to engage them and bring them alive to the
public discourse.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.050
And we at AEI are deeply grateful, not just
to tonight’s Irving Kristol Award winner

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.861
and his colleagues in elective public service,
but to so many of you here tonight who will

00:05:12.861 --> 00:05:17.410
put the fight for freedom, individual opportunity,
and free enterprise at the center of your

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.300
lives and at the center of your careers.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.380
At this time, it’s my honor, on behalf of
AEI’s Council of Economic Advisers and the

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:32.330
Kristol family, to introduce the winner of
this years’ Irving Kristol Award.

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:37.750
One time, not that long ago, a journalist
asked me this question, “As president of

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.300
AEI, you deal with a lot of politicians.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.310
Who is the think tankiest of them all?”

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:50.500
Now, by this, I think he meant the ability
to dive deeply into policy and not the tendency

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.880
to have soup on his tie.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.910
My reaction was immediate.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.880
Congressman Paul Ryan from Wisconsin.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.979
Few politicians can master the details of
policy like Paul.

00:06:04.979 --> 00:06:10.020
Furthermore, few can fit the details into
a policy message that dignifies the values

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.860
of America’s founders, and it’s just extraordinary.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.210
That conversation with a journalist got me
thinking about the Irving Kristol Award that

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:23.340
we present each year, and I nominated Paul
to AEI’s Council of Economic Advisers.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:27.110
They enthusiastically agreed to give him this
award.

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.400
That was last summer.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.759
When I called Paul to tell him, he gladly
accepted, but he offhandedly said that it

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:38.199
might be a bit complicated by a new turn of
events in his life.

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:43.910
A week later, Mitt Romney announced that Paul
was to be his running mate for vice president.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:45.630
What a year it’s been for Paul.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:51.580
I’m really delighted tonight to dedicate
an evening and AEI’s highest honor to Paul

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.410
Ryan’s service, his ideas, and his future.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:57.260
I can’t wait to hear his thoughts.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:02.639
Ladies and gentlemen, I give you the winner
of the 2013 Irving Kristol Award, Congressman

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:08.490
Paul Ryan.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:19.762
PAUL RYAN: Thank you.

00:07:19.762 --> 00:07:20.762
Wow.

00:07:20.762 --> 00:07:24.360
Thank you very much.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:33.330
Thank you very much.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:42.300
Really an undue honor.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.789
Thank you.

00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:55.289
You know, what do I say?

00:07:55.289 --> 00:07:57.960
I wanna thank Arthur for this, for his remarks.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:03.770
When he did call me that one day, that’s
not exactly what I said.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:09.180
I said, “I thought you didn't give this
thing to politicians.”

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.750
I wanna thank you all for this award.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:18.440
You know, as I look around this room, one
phrase just keeps coming to my mind: think

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:21.819
tank prom.

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:23.680
You all look so lovely.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.500
What a wonderful evening.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.910
And it’s an honor to be recognized by your
peers.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:33.339
It’s also a special honor to be recognized
for your work.

00:08:33.339 --> 00:08:39.779
So, I hope that this evening I can give you
some sustenance and I can do it in as brief

00:08:39.779 --> 00:08:43.940
a period of time as possible so you can get
on with your meal.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:49.480
I wanna say that I’m especially grateful
because in this particular case, it’s an

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:54.480
award to be recognized by your peers for your
contribution to a cause.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:01.139
It’s our common cause, and the cause that
we’ve labored with.

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:07.079
The cause of AEI is the American idea.

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:08.819
What is this idea?

00:09:08.819 --> 00:09:13.019
Well, it’s pretty simple.

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:20.420
It’s the belief that the circumstance of
your birth should not determine the outcome

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.800
of your life.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.839
That if you work hard, you play by the rules,
you get ahead, you can get ahead and make

00:09:26.839 --> 00:09:33.620
a difference.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.610
As I will explain, this belief is at risk.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.480
It’s in need of support.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.949
And AEI, AEI has given it support.

00:09:42.949 --> 00:09:47.610
For years, you’ve taught the country about
the American idea — what it means, where

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.670
it came from, who first spoke of it, and why.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:56.899
And one man who gave his all to this effort
was the man who we’re really honoring tonight,

00:09:56.899 --> 00:09:59.550
Irving Kristol.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.499
Irving Kristol was a renaissance man.

00:10:01.499 --> 00:10:05.949
He could discuss Russo and size up Reagan
all while the rest of us try to catch up with

00:10:05.949 --> 00:10:07.480
him.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:13.759
He was a perfect blend of book smarts, know-how;
in short, he was one of a kind.

00:10:13.759 --> 00:10:16.029
But you’ve carried on his work quite well
here at AEI.

00:10:16.029 --> 00:10:18.199
You’ve continued to defend the American
idea.

00:10:18.199 --> 00:10:22.360
I, too, have come to a number of these dinners.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:27.290
Four years ago, Charles Murray stood here
and argued that we should keep our way of

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.500
life because it’s the best way of life.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:33.120
It’s the most challenging and the most fulfilling.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.179
I was so moved by that lecture that I printed
off hard copies and sent it to every one of

00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:39.709
my staffs.

00:10:39.709 --> 00:10:41.170
Sent to anybody I could find.

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:44.930
Last year, Leon Kass took out the baton.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:50.379
He argued that we should love our country,
not just because it’s our own, but because

00:10:50.379 --> 00:10:51.920
it’s good.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.300
It respects every person’s dignity.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:01.269
Both men reminded us why we should defend
the American idea.

00:11:01.269 --> 00:11:03.199
But we might lose it.

00:11:03.199 --> 00:11:06.639
Today, the left runs Washington.

00:11:06.639 --> 00:11:09.709
They seek to replace the American idea with
the progressive state.

00:11:09.709 --> 00:11:14.199
They wanna replace equal opportunity with
equal outcomes.

00:11:14.199 --> 00:11:18.079
First, let me say they’re well-intentioned.

00:11:18.079 --> 00:11:22.279
They’re trying to do good as they see it.

00:11:22.279 --> 00:11:27.559
Hard as it might be to admit, they’re speaking
to a need, a need for security in a world

00:11:27.559 --> 00:11:31.040
of growing complexity.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:37.989
So before conservatives can win, we have to
understand what it is we’re doing wrong.

00:11:37.989 --> 00:11:43.149
The fact is, we also have to speak to this
need.

00:11:43.149 --> 00:11:47.939
We have to explain how too much government
will weaken security and how our agenda will

00:11:47.939 --> 00:11:49.459
increase security.

00:11:49.459 --> 00:11:52.069
We have to reclaim the center of our politics.

00:11:52.069 --> 00:11:55.720
And we can do this; it is not too late.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:12:01.019
My predecessors on this stage discussed why
we should save the American idea.

00:12:01.019 --> 00:12:04.869
Tonight, I want to discuss how we can save
the American idea.

00:12:04.869 --> 00:12:07.040
It’s a big project.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.100
It goes way beyond politics.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:14.199
But I will stick to the political side with
a due sense of humility in a crowd like this.

00:12:14.199 --> 00:12:17.139
Here’s the “CliffsNotes” version.

00:12:17.139 --> 00:12:23.809
Both the left and the right too often split
the world into halves — the individual and

00:12:23.809 --> 00:12:25.639
the government.

00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:30.079
They forget the key part of life, that part
that gives real security.

00:12:30.079 --> 00:12:34.239
They forget society, that space in-between.

00:12:34.239 --> 00:12:38.279
We can save the American idea by saving that
space for society.

00:12:38.279 --> 00:12:45.389
All in all, I hope that I show you the value
of a politician’s perspective and that after

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:48.929
I’m done, you don’t take the award back.

00:12:48.929 --> 00:12:55.660
First, let’s review where things stand.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:59.310
The left thinks we’re in a new era, and
for good reason.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:02.180
The health care law is not just another entitlement.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.689
It puts one-sixth of our economy in the hands
of federal bureaucrats.

00:13:06.689 --> 00:13:12.809
It allows government to stage-manage our lives
in the most personal of domains, our health.

00:13:12.809 --> 00:13:16.610
And now that the Supreme Court has uphold
the law, we cannot be sure that it will enforce

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:18.610
the Constitution’s limits.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:22.889
We can’t be sure that government will stay
within its boundaries.

00:13:22.889 --> 00:13:27.109
So, how do we get here?

00:13:27.109 --> 00:13:31.529
The health care law is part of a larger movement
called progressivism.

00:13:31.529 --> 00:13:34.209
It began in the late 19th century.

00:13:34.209 --> 00:13:36.629
At first, it was a bipartisan affair.

00:13:36.629 --> 00:13:40.360
The progressives included Republicans like
Teddy Roosevelt and Democrats like Woodrow

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:41.919
Wilson.

00:13:41.919 --> 00:13:44.870
These leaders were skeptical of the Constitution.

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:49.049
They disliked the idea of limited government,
and you can understand why.

00:13:49.049 --> 00:13:53.019
At the turn of the 20th century, change was
everywhere from the crowded streets in New

00:13:53.019 --> 00:13:56.339
York to the plains of Texas.

00:13:56.339 --> 00:13:59.660
America was becoming more urban, more industrial.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:03.730
Families were leaving the farm for the city
where their lives fell into turmoil, and life

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:06.370
became much more complex.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.889
No longer did most lives follow the changing
of the seasons.

00:14:09.889 --> 00:14:13.410
They now followed the twists and the turns
of the business cycle.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:16.880
We were growing fast, which meant serious
growing pains.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:21.319
Immigrants slept in tiny apartments, 10 to
a room.

00:14:21.319 --> 00:14:24.129
Families lived with a threat of disease and
often death.

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:25.129
Banks went bust.

00:14:25.129 --> 00:14:29.679
Our economy was growing mightily, but there
was great pain along the way.

00:14:29.679 --> 00:14:35.220
And that pain seemed to cry out for somebody
to do something.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.880
And that somebody, the progressives thought,
was the federal government.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:43.790
The progressives thought they were improving
on the founders’ work.

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:47.980
They thought that the Constitution was old
and inadequate.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:50.059
People needed more than just natural rights.

00:14:50.059 --> 00:14:52.799
People needed government-granted rights.

00:14:52.799 --> 00:14:55.629
Only government could navigate the turns of
history.

00:14:55.629 --> 00:14:59.100
Only government could remove the uncertainty
from life.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.739
In the progressive state, government would
build up the most wealth for our country and

00:15:02.739 --> 00:15:06.129
divvy it up in the fairest way.

00:15:06.129 --> 00:15:09.249
The progressives saw our federal system as
an obstacle.

00:15:09.249 --> 00:15:12.790
They thought our local communities were parochial
and inefficient.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:14.569
Why should people have to rely on their family?

00:15:14.569 --> 00:15:17.410
Why should they have to work with their neighbors?

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:21.791
They believed the attachments of family and
neighborhood, like the Constitution, were

00:15:21.791 --> 00:15:23.509
old and inadequate.

00:15:23.509 --> 00:15:27.100
Their policies weakened those attachments.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:31.899
In fact, they shrinked into one attachment,
attachment to the government.

00:15:31.899 --> 00:15:36.220
Now, the progressives wanted a national community
where governments stood supreme tending to

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:41.229
the needs of its subjects.

00:15:41.229 --> 00:15:47.540
Progressivism is well-intentioned, but it
is also, in my opinion, in my humble opinion,

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.399
arrogant and condescending.

00:15:49.399 --> 00:15:57.139
Instead of helping people make their own decisions,
it makes those decisions for them.

00:15:57.139 --> 00:16:01.380
It makes Washington the center of power and
politicians the center of attention.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:06.019
Here’s one reason Teddy Roosevelt was a
progressive.

00:16:06.019 --> 00:16:08.949
His daughter once said that he wanted to be
the bride at every wedding and the corpse

00:16:08.949 --> 00:16:13.699
at every funeral.

00:16:13.699 --> 00:16:16.259
But this vision proved compelling.

00:16:16.259 --> 00:16:20.919
It drew thousands of people into government
— the new dealers, the whiz kids, the poverty

00:16:20.919 --> 00:16:22.549
warriors.

00:16:22.549 --> 00:16:25.919
Confident in their cause, they seized the
moral high ground.

00:16:25.919 --> 00:16:31.220
They said they were the heirs of the founders
when, in reality, they were the replacements.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:32.499
They said they were for the people.

00:16:32.499 --> 00:16:35.439
And their opponents, well, they were for the
rich.

00:16:35.439 --> 00:16:37.519
They were selfish.

00:16:37.519 --> 00:16:41.350
When we were debating the health care bill
in the House, one Democrat described the Republican

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:42.749
position this way.

00:16:42.749 --> 00:16:44.119
He said, “Don’t get sick.

00:16:44.119 --> 00:16:45.600
And if you do, die quickly.”

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:51.669
Funny, I don’t seem to remember that part
in our substitute bill.

00:16:51.669 --> 00:16:54.249
The progressives hijacked our rhetoric as
well.

00:16:54.249 --> 00:16:57.019
They knew the appeal of our founding principles.

00:16:57.019 --> 00:17:01.459
They masked their novel ideas in the founders’
language and still do.

00:17:01.459 --> 00:17:07.549
As President Woodrow Wilson started new federal
agencies and created a new income tax, what

00:17:07.549 --> 00:17:09.699
did he call his agenda?

00:17:09.699 --> 00:17:13.550
The New Freedom.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:20.180
Behind all of this talk is the same idea,
the same idea behind the health care law.

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:22.909
The left thinks they can make health care
more rational.

00:17:22.909 --> 00:17:25.919
And they don’t mind stepping on a few toes
to do it.

00:17:25.919 --> 00:17:28.230
The law puts new burdens on doctors.

00:17:28.230 --> 00:17:33.309
It adds new coverage mandates, including those
that violate some people’s religious beliefs.

00:17:33.309 --> 00:17:38.860
So, doctors talk of closing their offices,
and Catholic bishops are thinking of closing

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:41.330
their hospitals.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.289
Government is pushing out all those providers
who don’t agree with it.

00:17:44.289 --> 00:17:48.250
It’s clearing out that space between itself
and each person.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:54.710
It’s invading deeply personal relationships,
and in some cases, ending them.

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:58.269
Yet they keep winning elections.

00:17:58.269 --> 00:17:59.769
Why?

00:17:59.769 --> 00:18:03.480
Well, you can see the appeal.

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:08.600
In uncertain times, people look for security.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.679
Progressives seem to have an answer.

00:18:11.679 --> 00:18:17.070
We may not live in the farms anymore, but
we are moving into an information-driven economy

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:21.190
where change is so rapid.

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:25.240
Creative destruction sounds a lot better than
it feels.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.000
Change dislocates and disrupts.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.549
The hardships are real.

00:18:28.549 --> 00:18:32.809
In the progressive state, it offers a sense
of security.

00:18:32.809 --> 00:18:38.620
But it’s a false sense of security, because
government cannot keep all of these promises.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:41.350
We’re learning this the hard way.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:44.380
For years, we’ve talked about big government
in theory.

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:47.889
Now, we’re seeing it in practice.

00:18:47.889 --> 00:18:50.710
Again, look at the health care law.

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:54.409
We were told, “If you like your insurance
plan, you can keep it.”

00:18:54.409 --> 00:18:56.570
But insurance companies are dropping coverage.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:58.600
Companies are dropping their coverage.

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.370
We were told that if you liked your doctor
you could keep her, but your doctor may not

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.100
keep you.

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:07.830
We were told that premiums will fall, but
they’re going up dramatically.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:14.059
I believe this health care law will collapse
under its own weight, but we have to offer

00:19:14.059 --> 00:19:16.789
something better in its place.

00:19:16.789 --> 00:19:19.809
This is our opportunity to take back the initiative.

00:19:19.809 --> 00:19:26.750
And our goal, let me be really clear about
this, our goal is not simply to win an election,

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:40.380
it’s to improve people’s lives.

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:42.320
Politics is a means to an end.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:46.259
It’s not an end in and of itself.

00:19:46.259 --> 00:19:50.750
And the end is for all people to be able to
pursue happiness.

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:59.490
So, our job is not to make even more empty
promises; it’s to revive the American idea.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:04.129
We have to show that the American idea is
superior to the progressive state, both in

00:20:04.129 --> 00:20:07.779
our time and for all time.

00:20:07.779 --> 00:20:14.570
We have to show that the American idea offers
true security because unlike the progressive

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:18.129
state, it offers true community.

00:20:18.129 --> 00:20:19.809
Its promise is real.

00:20:19.809 --> 00:20:23.480
Here’s what the left got right.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:28.480
The American idea needs a strong government
to secure it, but a government is effective

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:30.100
only when it is limited.

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:34.679
And a massive government can stifle the American
idea.

00:20:34.679 --> 00:20:38.690
Government can’t replace our local communities,
and it shouldn’t even try.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:42.809
Instead, it should reinforce our communities.

00:20:42.809 --> 00:20:47.820
Government should expand the space where a
free society can thrive.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:52.039
We should expand that space because it’s
part of the American idea.

00:20:52.039 --> 00:20:56.000
We want everyone to have a chance in life,
a chance to be happy.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.000
And you know what?

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:58.580
We’re happiest when we’re together.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:20:59.889
We want to be together.

00:20:59.889 --> 00:21:00.929
It’s in our nature.

00:21:00.929 --> 00:21:05.200
We feel it in our bones.

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:11.950
Now, Barney Frank once said, “Government
is the name for the things we do together.”

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:14.830
That’s just one name.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:20.250
There are lots of them: the church meeting,
the neighborhood watch, the food bank, the

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:24.240
small business, the health care clinic, the
homeless shelter.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:31.630
We call these things mediating institutions,
but, in the end, they’re just people, people

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.750
working together.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:40.000
And the more we work together, out of our
own free will, the stronger we become.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:44.970
The strongest glue isn’t fear or force,
it’s friendship and love.

00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:49.580
We stick together.

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:56.000
We stick together because we share the same
beliefs.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.039
That’s the source of our strength.

00:21:58.039 --> 00:22:02.580
And when government tries to do too much,
when it replaces cooperation with coercion,

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:04.100
it weakens our country.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:05.220
It pulls us apart.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.879
It deprives people of their purpose.

00:22:07.879 --> 00:22:12.840
So, conservatives, most of all, believe in
cooperation because, most of all, we believe

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:14.669
in freedom.

00:22:14.669 --> 00:22:20.520
That’s something I learned from AEI, from
people like Peter Berger, Richard Newhouse,

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.360
Michael Novak, Bill Schambra.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:27.330
You’ve preserved and expanded on Robert
Nesbitt's central insight.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:32.970
People hunger for freedom, for self-fulfillment,
and they hunger for a community where they

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:34.509
can realize their potential.

00:22:34.509 --> 00:22:37.131
That’s the key to the American idea, and
I learned it from you.

00:22:37.131 --> 00:22:50.700
You know, I also learned it from my mom, Betty.

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:58.000
Thanks for the shout-out for Betty.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:22:59.789
My dad died when I was 16.

00:22:59.789 --> 00:23:04.899
It was tough on our family, but my mom went
out, and she got involved.

00:23:04.899 --> 00:23:09.950
She took flight, she got ready, she pulled
herself up.

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:13.730
She got involved in everything: the school
board, the local parish, the garden club,

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:14.840
the bridge club.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:17.950
Heck, she didn’t even know how to play bridge.

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:21.100
She still doesn’t.

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:23.029
And she made lifelong friends.

00:23:23.029 --> 00:23:25.070
In a group of widows, actually.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:28.799
When a friend in Janesville would lose a husband,
Betty would be the first there to comfort.

00:23:28.799 --> 00:23:32.030
And over time, they formed a group, and it
began to grow.

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:35.240
Out of their losses, they created something
new.

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:37.010
They formed a community of support.

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.250
She’s just one of many people like her.

00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:43.990
My hometown, Janesville, Wisconsin, is full
of people like this.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:46.559
Like a guy named Burdette Erickson.

00:23:46.559 --> 00:23:51.610
Several years ago, the fourth ward of Janesville
was overrun with drug dealers, and they were

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:52.830
brazen.

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:57.860
They did their deals in the light of day,
even on one elderly woman’s front porch.

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:02.730
She had to close her front drapes for fear
of seeing what was happening, and she was

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:04.140
worried she would get shot.

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:06.749
This is in Janesville, Wisconsin.

00:24:06.749 --> 00:24:11.559
So Burdette gathered his neighbors in his
basement one night, and he and his neighbors,

00:24:11.559 --> 00:24:13.190
they made a pact with one another.

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:16.370
Either the drug dealers go, or we go.

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.299
They formed a neighborhood watch.

00:24:18.299 --> 00:24:22.529
Their families told the police about the gangs’
deals and their hideouts.

00:24:22.529 --> 00:24:26.230
And soon, they took their neighborhood back.

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:30.330
Now the inmates in our local prison in Janesville,
they tell the people coming through, “Don’t

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:33.700
do drugs here, don’t deal, you’ll get
busted.”

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:45.330
And so young families are moving in, and the
drug dealers are moving out.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:52.220
Our country is full of stories like these,
of people banding together to meet a common

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:53.220
need.

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:58.580
And the most obvious example of this is our
system of free enterprise.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:03.760
You know, as Arthur likes to say, “We have
to remember free enterprise isn’t only the

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:09.940
efficient thing to do, it’s also the right
thing to do because it’s a school of character.

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:14.120
The voluntary exchange of goods and services
brings out the best in us.

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:15.470
It builds trust.

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:16.470
It teaches discipline.

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.129
And it rewards hard work.

00:25:19.129 --> 00:25:22.720
We have to make the moral case for free enterprise.”

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:27.490
In short, we have to show the full scope of
our vision.

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:31.720
We have to explain that conservatism is about
more than the economy; it’s also about our

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:32.720
culture.

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:35.289
It’s about the kind of country we wanna
be.

00:25:35.289 --> 00:25:39.350
It’s about the kind of life we wanna share.

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:45.279
We want people to enjoy the journey of living
a full life, a life full of trials and tribulations,

00:25:45.279 --> 00:25:48.509
of loss and gain, and, ultimately, of the
betterment of ourselves, our children, and

00:25:48.509 --> 00:25:51.530
our communities.

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:56.929
Here’s our problem.

00:25:56.929 --> 00:26:01.889
We have failed to communicate this vision
to those who have never heard of it.

00:26:01.889 --> 00:26:08.700
We have retreated to our cultural cul-de-sacs
in order to protect our immediate surroundings.

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:17.190
Meanwhile, our inner cities, our barrios,
our poor rural communities are languishing.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.580
This is where our opportunity lies.

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:21.610
This is where we must go.

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:34.130
This is where we must demonstrate our full
vision of freedom and community.

00:26:34.130 --> 00:26:38.230
This vision is our response to progressivism.

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:44.830
It’s not as easy to sell, but it’s more
complete, and it’s much more real.

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:49.730
We have to show how it works and how, in so
many cases, today’s version of the American

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:52.710
idea is right under our noses.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:53.710
You know what?

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:56.260
We can start just six miles down the road
from here.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:27:01.120
A few weeks ago, I took a trip to Anacostia
with my good friend, Bob Woodson.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:05.539
I’m a really big fan of Bob Woodson’s
and a big believer in his Center for Neighborhood

00:27:05.539 --> 00:27:07.999
Enterprises.

00:27:07.999 --> 00:27:17.019
You see, Bob just doesn’t talk about these
communities; he talks with these communities.

00:27:17.019 --> 00:27:22.460
In fact, it is in these very neighborhoods
that we see our vision in action.

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:27.159
Tonight, I wanna share a story that I heard
just six miles from here.

00:27:27.159 --> 00:27:30.990
It’s about a man named James Woods.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:32.559
James grew up around here.

00:27:32.559 --> 00:27:33.789
His family wasn’t well-off.

00:27:33.789 --> 00:27:37.440
They struggled, but they loved each other.

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:42.139
He served 10 years in the military, and after
he got out, he met up with some friends from

00:27:42.139 --> 00:27:45.070
his past.

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:47.419
Those friends got him in trouble.

00:27:47.419 --> 00:27:50.020
They started selling drugs.

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:52.010
He became addicted.

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:53.350
He was homeless.

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:54.690
He slept in the streets.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:57.809
Pretty soon, the law caught up with him.

00:27:57.809 --> 00:28:01.380
James was charged with selling drugs.

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:06.100
The law said 20 years in prison, but the judge
gave him a break.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:07.700
He got only three.

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:13.499
After James got out of prison, he made a decision.

00:28:13.499 --> 00:28:17.090
He wanted to be the man that his parents raised
him to be.

00:28:17.090 --> 00:28:18.769
He would change.

00:28:18.769 --> 00:28:23.559
So he joined an all men’s ministry, but
he was still struggling.

00:28:23.559 --> 00:28:24.990
He was still searching.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:32.499
And every night, through that bedroom window,
he would hear this singing.

00:28:32.499 --> 00:28:36.190
It was in the ministry of Pastor Shirley Holloway
right next door.

00:28:36.190 --> 00:28:41.590
He didn’t know Pastor Holloway, but he heard
her members praying.

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:42.730
He was intrigued.

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:51.049
So, one day, he went across the street, and
he joined the House of Help, the City of Hope.

00:28:51.049 --> 00:28:53.169
It was a life-changing moment.

00:28:53.169 --> 00:28:57.259
James became good friends with Pastor Holloway,
and she did him a big favor.

00:28:57.259 --> 00:29:00.659
She paid his legal fees to keep him out of
jail.

00:29:00.659 --> 00:29:01.659
James got a job.

00:29:01.659 --> 00:29:07.369
He offered to pay her back, but instead of
cashing his checks, Pastor Holloway was saving

00:29:07.369 --> 00:29:12.899
them so that he would ultimately have a nest
egg.

00:29:12.899 --> 00:29:14.690
He was stunned.

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.950
It wasn’t that she saved his money without
his knowing.

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:22.340
It wasn’t that she gave him a job.

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:26.049
It was that she showed him love.

00:29:26.049 --> 00:29:29.850
James would say that he didn’t expect Pastor
Holloway’s love because she didn’t know

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:34.600
him, but in a way, she did know James.

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:36.890
She knew who he could be.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:40.990
Soon, he turned his life around.

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:44.629
At the ministry, he met his future wife, Angela.

00:29:44.629 --> 00:29:46.440
She followed a path similar to James.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:50.740
And today, they’ve been married for 13 years.

00:29:50.740 --> 00:29:56.500
As James would say, “Angela loves God now,”
and because she’s his wife, “She’s always

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:57.500
praying.”

00:29:57.500 --> 00:30:02.710
He’s been clean for 13 years.

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:06.270
He now counsels about 60 men at the ministry.

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:07.270
He helps the unemployed.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:08.270
He helps the homeless.

00:30:08.270 --> 00:30:09.320
He helps the addicted.

00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:15.880
Angela, meanwhile, has a steady job as a security
guard.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:21.500
If you asked Pastor Holloway for her secret,
she would say her ministry uses two ingredients:

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:23.910
faith and love.

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:28.409
Her motto is, “We don’t see the problem,
we see the person.”

00:30:28.409 --> 00:30:34.249
Since the ministry started in 1995, they’ve
served over 40,000 people, six miles from

00:30:34.249 --> 00:30:36.210
here, struggling with drug abuse.

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.820
Eighty-five percent of their members have
stayed clean.

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:39.820
Eighty-five percent.

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:45.769
The secret, of course, it’s the people.

00:30:45.769 --> 00:30:50.169
In people, we find real security and real
love.

00:30:50.169 --> 00:30:53.029
A welfare check would not have helped James.

00:30:53.029 --> 00:30:55.980
It might’ve met his material needs, but
only for a time.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:58.980
He had spiritual needs too.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.429
Only people and God can address those needs.

00:31:02.429 --> 00:31:08.990
James, Angela, and Pastor Holloway, they’re
three great examples.

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:12.159
And I’m honored to have him here as my guests
tonight.

00:31:12.159 --> 00:31:47.009
Please join me in recognizing them and thanking
them for their inspiration and their witness.

00:31:47.009 --> 00:31:52.480
When we make policy, we should keep people
like James in mind.

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:56.830
Our job is not to replace the Pastor Holloways;
it’s to support them.

00:31:56.830 --> 00:32:01.340
Yes, the federal government has a role to
play, but it’s a supporting role, not a

00:32:01.340 --> 00:32:02.990
leading one.

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:07.130
Its job is to give people the resources and
the space to thrive.

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:14.779
And in this role, we should follow two principles:
solidarity and subsidiarity.

00:32:14.779 --> 00:32:19.409
Solidarity is the belief that we’re all
in this together, that we share a common purpose,

00:32:19.409 --> 00:32:21.460
the pursuit of happiness.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:26.239
And public servants should share one goal,
the common good.

00:32:26.239 --> 00:32:29.830
Subsidiarity, it’s like federalism.

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:34.850
It’s the belief that each part of society
adds to the whole and that each part must

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:41.669
be free to do its work on its own terms, so
government shouldn’t assume other people’s

00:32:41.669 --> 00:32:42.690
tasks.

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:46.260
It shouldn’t make decisions better left
with the family or the neighborhood.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:49.740
The people closest to the problem, they’re
more likely to solve it because they know

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:51.059
the community best.

00:32:51.059 --> 00:33:00.049
And this is the opposite of progressivism,
which believes that Washington knows best.

00:33:00.049 --> 00:33:03.110
We need to apply these timeless principles
to the challenges of the day.

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:05.379
You know, that’s what we do in the House
budget.

00:33:05.379 --> 00:33:08.260
What’s the link here?

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:13.200
Our budget is known for one part of our vision,
that government can’t spend beyond its means.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:16.260
And it’s true, the national debt, it hurts
our economy.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:17.470
It restricts opportunity.

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:19.820
It weighs down our communities.

00:33:19.820 --> 00:33:21.899
We have to stop spending money we don’t
have.

00:33:21.899 --> 00:33:26.379
But that’s our policy; it’s not our purpose.

00:33:26.379 --> 00:33:29.399
Our purpose is to reclaim the American idea.

00:33:29.399 --> 00:33:32.489
And our policies reflect that purpose.

00:33:32.489 --> 00:33:38.159
We’re not just trying to balance the books;
we’re trying to grow the economy.

00:33:38.159 --> 00:33:41.330
We’re trying to expand that space for society.

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:46.580
The welfare state, well, it threatens to close
that space.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:49.659
That’s why we need to change course.

00:33:49.659 --> 00:33:52.490
We need to strike a balance between society
and governments.

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:55.429
We need to let each part play its role.

00:33:55.429 --> 00:33:58.980
We need government to meet its obligations
without crowding out the American idea.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:05.419
In short, our purpose is to ensure that if
you work hard and play by the rules, you can

00:34:05.419 --> 00:34:06.419
get ahead.

00:34:06.419 --> 00:34:10.369
It’s to leave our children a country as
strong as the one we inherited.

00:34:10.369 --> 00:34:14.450
And to do that, government must reinforce
the space for society.

00:34:14.450 --> 00:34:19.119
It must apply the principles of solidarity
and subsidiarity.

00:34:19.119 --> 00:34:20.929
Take Medicare.

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:23.100
We need to protect this critical program.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:28.780
We need to maintain solidarity with our seniors,
but we also need to strengthen it by harnessing

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:30.210
the knowledge of our local communities.

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:33.500
We need to harness the power of subsidiarity.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:38.230
Instead of imposing one plan on everyone,
we give seniors a choice so they can pick

00:34:38.230 --> 00:34:41.580
the insurance plan that works best for them.

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:44.240
Progressives think people aren’t smart enough
to make these decisions on their own, they

00:34:44.240 --> 00:34:46.060
shouldn’t be given these options.

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:48.659
But look at Medicare’s prescription drug
benefit.

00:34:48.659 --> 00:34:51.220
It works like the reform we’d apply to all
of Medicare.

00:34:51.220 --> 00:34:57.020
It comes in consistently under budget, and
satisfaction with that program is sky high.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:59.820
We apply the same principles to Medicaid.

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:05.609
We maintain funding to show solidarity with
the poor, and we harness the power of subsidiarity

00:35:05.609 --> 00:35:08.160
by enlisting the aid of the states.

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:10.609
We give state governments more flexibility.

00:35:10.609 --> 00:35:14.200
We let them tailor their programs to meet
their people’s needs.

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:21.200
And by doing so, we hope to make Medicaid
a better program for those who rely on it,

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:23.570
but our replacement needs to go further than
that.

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:29.890
We have to help people who aren’t on either
program, and that’s the majority of the

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:30.890
country.

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:34.550
The health care law will make things much
worse, but even the current health care system

00:35:34.550 --> 00:35:36.030
needs to be fixed.

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:38.410
That is something we all must acknowledge.

00:35:38.410 --> 00:35:44.250
Today, our tax code provides an open-ended
subsidy for an employer’s health insurance.

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:47.200
It does nothing for people who buy health
insurance on their own.

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:52.330
The code locks workers to their jobs, it favors
the wealthy, and it pushes up costs.

00:35:52.330 --> 00:35:56.619
Well, we need to help families get and keep
their health insurance.

00:35:56.619 --> 00:36:01.020
We can do that by attaching the tax benefit
to the individual.

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:06.019
And if they choose a plan that’s less expensive
than the benefit, then they get a refund.

00:36:06.019 --> 00:36:08.750
People shouldn’t have to lose their insurance
if they change jobs.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:11.930
The benefits should travel with the person,
not with the job.

00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:14.490
Here’s the big question.

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:18.049
Who should decide?

00:36:18.049 --> 00:36:21.499
We think you should decide, not Washington.

00:36:21.499 --> 00:36:25.650
Under our plan, the federal government would
make a defined contribution to your health

00:36:25.650 --> 00:36:26.770
care security.

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:32.970
We’d cap the rate of growth of that contribution
to eliminate waste, to encourage competition,

00:36:32.970 --> 00:36:37.550
but we would also give more help to the poor
and the sick, less help to the rich.

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:39.520
Support would go to only those who needed
it.

00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:43.880
And under our plan, we would put you in control.

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:47.020
Only you know what works best for you and
your family.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:52.370
Our reforms should offer people not just the
dignity of self-determination, but the comfort

00:36:52.370 --> 00:36:54.579
of community.

00:36:54.579 --> 00:36:58.280
Health care, health care is a deeply personal
issue.

00:36:58.280 --> 00:37:01.800
When your health care is at stake, you want
your doctor to be someone you trust.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:06.190
You want somebody you know, someone who knows
you.

00:37:06.190 --> 00:37:10.750
In Janesville, Janna and I are more likely
to see our doctors at the YMCA or at the school

00:37:10.750 --> 00:37:14.730
play then at the clinic, just as it can and
should be.

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:20.599
You don’t want be just the next person in
a line.

00:37:20.599 --> 00:37:25.349
You want the doctor to be like family, but
the health care law is forcing doctors to

00:37:25.349 --> 00:37:26.770
close their practices.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:30.660
It’s taking this relationship out of our
hands.

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:35.740
And if we reform health care the right way,
free enterprise can control costs and increase

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:42.740
quality without this kind of bullying.

00:37:42.740 --> 00:37:49.700
Markets aren’t bleak tundras where the strong
dominate the weak as progressives all too

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:50.900
often imagine.

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:53.530
They’re pipelines of knowledge.

00:37:53.530 --> 00:37:56.970
They bring crucial decisions to the family
where they belong.

00:37:56.970 --> 00:37:58.540
And it’s not just health care.

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:05.890
We have to apply these principles to all the
challenges of the day: defense, energy, education,

00:38:05.890 --> 00:38:07.809
immigration, taxes.

00:38:07.809 --> 00:38:12.910
We have to explain how our policies will improve
people’s lives.

00:38:12.910 --> 00:38:17.109
The answer is, we will use the power and the
resources of the government to give people

00:38:17.109 --> 00:38:18.720
the room to thrive.

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:23.170
We will maintain our partnership our society
has had with our government in the past even

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:28.050
in this new era of an aging society and a
global economy.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:32.750
This, this is a complete vision of conservatism.

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:34.920
It’s what we’re striving for.

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:37.010
It’s not a vision of petty materialism.

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:41.420
It’s not one of lonely individuals overseen
by a massive government.

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:50.220
It’s one of moral nourishment, of self-fulfillment,
of growth, and opportunity.

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:53.000
We can’t treat politics like a game.

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:54.809
We aren’t competing for a trophy.

00:38:54.809 --> 00:38:57.260
We’re competing over the country’s future.

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:01.349
We’re trying to determine what kind of people
we’re going to be.

00:39:01.349 --> 00:39:03.339
We have to recognize the stakes.

00:39:03.339 --> 00:39:08.609
We have to get serious, and I believe we will.

00:39:08.609 --> 00:39:14.319
Winston Churchill once said, “The Americans
can be counted upon to do the right thing,

00:39:14.319 --> 00:39:20.200
only after they’ve exhausted all the other
possibilities.”

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:21.230
He got it right.

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:23.849
Sometimes other people can see us better than
we see ourselves.

00:39:23.849 --> 00:39:29.140
In fact, I think the best description of the
American spirit was written by a Frenchman,

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:31.250
Alexis de Tocqueville.

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:34.480
We remember Tocqueville for his keen study
of our culture.

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:38.210
We remember his insight that our local communities
are schools of freedom.

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:41.559
They teach us how to see past our differences.

00:39:41.559 --> 00:39:43.150
They teach us how to work together.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:46.170
But he also had a sense for politics.

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:51.270
He said that the Senate was home to “the
celebrities of America,” that there was

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:55.290
hardly a member who hadn’t done an illustrious
deed.

00:39:55.290 --> 00:40:03.170
The House, on the other hand, was home to
“obscure persons, often the eye seeks in

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:05.900
vain for a celebrated man within it.”

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:09.300
It’s kind of humbling, you know.

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:16.970
On a night like this, I’m less eager to
stand out and much more eager to join in.

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:21.140
To receive the Kristol Award is to join a
fellowship of scholars.

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:24.520
It’s to take part in the community of ideas.

00:40:24.520 --> 00:40:27.920
You’re the people who got me interested
in politics.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:31.599
These scholars, they taught me how to take
it seriously.

00:40:31.599 --> 00:40:35.140
So I’m grateful, and I’m grateful for
tonight.

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:38.550
You’re quite the crowd for an evening’s
company.

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:44.050
And your dedication to truth and your pursuit
of justice, you’re a great testament to

00:40:44.050 --> 00:40:45.119
the American idea.

00:40:45.119 --> 00:40:46.520
Thank you.

00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:46.949
Thank you very much.

